Merges all phyloseq objects from a \link{list_phyloseq} into a \strong{single}
phyloseq object while \strong{keeping every sample separate}. Taxa are
unified \emph{across} objects by clustering their reference sequences
(\code{refseq} slot) with a clustering algorithm (vsearch by default), so
that sequences that are similar enough (controlled by \code{id}) become a
single merged taxon whose counts are summed within each original
sample.

This differs from \code{\link[=merge_lpq]{merge_lpq()}}, which collapses each phyloseq object
into a single sample and matches taxa by \strong{exact} sequence identity.
Use \code{merge_clust_lpq()} when you want to pool the raw samples of
several independently built datasets into one object and reconcile
their ASVs/OTUs through fuzzy (identity-threshold) clustering.

Samples keep their original names; when the same sample name occurs in
more than one object, the parent object name is appended as a suffix
(\verb{<sample>_<object>}) to keep sample names unique. A column
(\code{source_name} by default) recording the parent object is added to the
\code{sample_data}.
}
